#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2ca6901-556b-5960-a3bd-8d0552587579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2ca6901-556b-5960-a3bd-8d0552587579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307252} :answer-field/id #uuid "09352193-2085-5ed0-9dac-3b45c1b2f367" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307553} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2961f6c2-30e1-5479-98d1-86e05fd1e48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2961f6c2-30e1-5479-98d1-86e05fd1e48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305541} :answer-field/id #uuid "743ad797-c4e6-5a90-81e7-9503d1668174" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eff0b088-3db3-5966-a1d7-4ea54d40635c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333715" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t) &={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t) &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50015ef5-0e92-5106-aea9-ab330a93796e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558019} {:answer/id #uuid "b590d98d-384a-59d4-bdd6-f31990d7de3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558229} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd659a-e0ec-5349-a757-959b558799ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558261} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a149ea-0805-59fc-962e-46dd8b99f2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558294} {:answer/id #uuid "e5478b07-ac20-593b-879f-47d35b336561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & {e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd659a-e0ec-5349-a757-959b558799ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558261} :answer-field/id #uuid "543370ed-7818-5d7f-878c-1a15d0e76c82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a83f3a56-7f37-59d6-8ed6-dd0cc81608ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-334302" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aec3636-0b05-53aa-b49f-bb68ba34e051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aec3636-0b05-53aa-b49f-bb68ba34e051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557927} :answer-field/id #uuid "46deef4f-e56b-5e36-8314-39c65e6b0ff6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f82863e-1510-5b78-bcdf-021a61eb750f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f82863e-1510-5b78-bcdf-021a61eb750f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557898} :answer-field/id #uuid "964568c1-49f6-591e-9282-96ed07b39888" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4742a9e2-e4be-5551-9318-6d3fbbd26726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4742a9e2-e4be-5551-9318-6d3fbbd26726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558007} :answer-field/id #uuid "beaac546-5dc7-52af-aedb-cbfc055afecd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad67dc0e-85fd-5faa-84ba-66c09b001f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad67dc0e-85fd-5faa-84ba-66c09b001f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558211} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9189462-1f98-510b-b2f5-d3d5e93103dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2ad2fef-a91c-56d1-9362-f2fbd4a52e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-334463" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(2t)={e}^{2t} \\\\ r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t} \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{5{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{5t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2t\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}}{3t}+\\frac{5{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{5t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t} \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5t\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}}{3t}+\\frac{5{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{5t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :