ank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a7c02c-3d69-5ee9-b2d1-6e74980ea60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9a7c02c-3d69-5ee9-b2d1-6e74980ea60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558337} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc546b2a-f18f-5a02-9cc9-a4dfb2de1de2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c0f5df5-2d8b-5cef-a048-d87044456178" :question/math-academy-id "q-334464" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-t\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-t)={e}^{-t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtract the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-t}}{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-t}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the diagonal entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2t}-{e}^{-t}}{3t}\\cdot t+\\frac{{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-t}}{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c339771-09ba-5022-9518-b108d54d7a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c339771-09ba-5022-9518-b108d54d7a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305572} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd870b2-a7c5-5395-803e-55ce4d4f4943" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307713} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a803fbc-355e-5705-83ad-dd81e32fcabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a803fbc-355e-5705-83ad-dd81e32fcabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305395} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6b70cd-fe87-553d-9371-7a3f6e38337f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307763} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbc416a2-fcf3-5f9c-b6c8-072133cbba6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc416a2-fcf3-5f9c-b6c8-072133cbba6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307041} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b8505f-4ff7-51cd-8b58-a89db40093f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7fee4a-c580-56e8-ad35-f8a8f1d5812e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f7fee4a-c580-56e8-ad35-f8a8f1d5812e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306213} :answer-field/id #uuid "f547f3a4-4a17-530c-9994-4e346ffc2d43" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "691520cf-4380-50be-8b5c-ac8a181c7472" :question/math-academy-id "q-334499" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(5t) &={e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(5t) &={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5t{e}^{5t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}(1-5t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{5t}t+{e}^{5t}(1-5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{5t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]}
